Here's the deal: every measurement you write down is only as good as the tool you used to get it. Significant figures (or "sig figs" for short) are the digits in a number that actually carry meaning about how precisely you measured something. {{viz:formula-01}}

So how do you count them? A few simple rules:
- Any non-zero digit always counts (1, 2, 3... all the way to 9).
- Zeros stuck between other digits count too (like the 0 in 405).
- Trailing zeros only count if there's a decimal point (100.0 has 4 sig figs, but plain old 100 might only have 1—ambiguous!).
- Leading zeros never count (0.0025 has just 2 sig figs—the 2 and the 5).

Watch what this looks like in practice. {{viz:3d-01}}

Why does any of this matter? Because when you're doing calculations, your answer can only be as precise as your least precise measurement. If you multiply a super precise number by a rough one, your answer inherits the roughness—you can't magically create precision that wasn't there to begin with. {{viz:anim-01}}

See how the count of significant digits shifts as a number changes. {{viz:chart-01}}

Getting comfortable with sig figs takes a little practice, but once it clicks, you'll never write down a number without thinking about what it's really telling you. {{viz:text-01}} Try it out for yourself. {{viz:simulation-01}}
